window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-06","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-06-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-01/","title_he":"א – ברכות חמשת מיני דגן","title_en":"The Blessings over the Five Species of Grain","body_en":"<p>The Sages instituted special blessings for the five species of grain, which are: wheat, barley, spelt, rye and oats.</p><p>In general, the five species of grain are annual plants, and if so, seemingly their blessing ought to have been \"Borei Peri Ha-adama\" (\"who creates the fruit of the ground\"); however, because of their importance, since the main part of a person's food – bread and cooked dishes – is made from them, the Sages instituted a special blessing for them, in order to thank God, may He be blessed, in a detailed manner for our main sustenance. Over bread: \"Ha-motzi leḥem min ha-aretz\" (\"who brings forth bread from the earth\"), and over cakes and cooked grain dishes: \"Borei minei mezonot\" (\"who creates various kinds of nourishment\").</p><p>In greater detail, the five species of grain can be eaten in five states, and in each state their blessing changes. Let us enumerate the five states:</p><p><b>1)</b> One who eats raw kernels of wheat or barley, since they have not yet been elevated to their choice state, recites \"Borei Peri Ha-adama.\" As for the after-blessing, there is a doubt (as explained in section 13).</p><p><b>2)</b> One who eats flour of ground grain, since he ate them in their very worst form, they have descended from their original stature, and he recites over them the simplest blessing: \"Shehakol nihya bi-dvaro\" (\"by whose word all things came to be\") (SA 208:5).</p><p><b>3)</b> If one cooked the grain kernels, such as groats, bulgur and Quaker oats, or made from their flour kinds of cooked dishes such as noodles and ptitim (pasta flakes) or pashtidot (baked casseroles), since they have been elevated to the level of a cooked dish that sustains the heart, he recites \"Borei minei mezonot.\" And after he has eaten a kezayit of them (about half a kebeitza), he recites Al Ha-miḥya, which is a Me'ein Shalosh blessing (a blessing that is an abridgement of the three blessings of Birkat Ha-mazon) (SA 208:2).</p><p><b>4)</b> If one kneaded the flour, baked it and made bread from it, he recites \"Ha-motzi leḥem min ha-aretz\" at the beginning and Birkat Ha-mazon at the end (168:9).</p><p><b>5)</b> There is one more kind which has an intermediate status, between a cooked grain dish and bread, and that is pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin. That is, kinds of baked goods such as cakes and crackers, which on the one hand are baked goods like bread, and on the other hand people are not accustomed to establish a meal (keviat se'uda) on them as on bread, but rather to eat them as dessert. The Sages determined that if one ate them in the way people eat between meals, for pleasure or to calm one's hunger, since he does not intend to be satiated as in a regular meal, his eating does not have the stature of eating bread, and therefore he should recite Mezonot before them and Al Ha-miḥya after them. But if he established a meal on them, their law is like that of bread, and he must wash his hands with a blessing before them, and recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over them. All this is because they are baked goods; but if one establishes his meal on noodles and ptitim, since they are cooked dishes and not baked goods, he always recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya over them.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-06-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-02/","title_he":"ב – מהי פת-הבאה-בכיסנים","title_en":"What Is Pat Ha-ba'a Be-kisnin?","body_en":"<p>Three explanations are mentioned in the words of the Rishonim for the kinds of pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin (whose laws were explained at the end of the previous section). <b>1)</b> Kinds of filled baked goods, such as hamantaschen and strudel. They are called pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin because the dough is a pocket (kis) that is filled with sweet things such as poppy seeds, cocoa and nuts (Rabbeinu Ḥananel). <b>2)</b> Kinds of baked goods to whose dough a flavor was added that creates a difference between them and bread, for example tortes or biscuits that have a sweet taste; or malawaḥ, to whose dough a lot of oil is added (Rambam). <b>3)</b> Kinds of hard baked goods that a person crunches (koses) with his teeth, such as pretzels, crackers and matzot (Rav Hai Gaon).</p><p>In practice, we are accustomed to regard all three of these types as pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin, their common denominator being that on the one hand they are baked goods like bread, and on the other hand people usually eat them casually. Therefore, as long as one does not establish a meal on them, one recites Mezonot before them and Al Ha-miḥya after them, and when one establishes a meal on them, their law is like bread in every respect (SA 168:7).<sup>1</sup></p><p>Sometimes a person intends to eat a little of a Mezonot baked good (pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin), and therefore he recites Mezonot at the beginning, but after he started eating he was drawn in and ate more and more until he ate the amount that constitutes establishing a meal; then, even though he recited Mezonot at the beginning, at the end he should recite Birkat Ha-mazon. And if, after tasting the Mezonot food, he decided to establish a meal on it, if what he intends to eat from now on contains the amount that constitutes establishing a meal, he should wash his hands and recite Ha-motzi. And if what he intends to eat afterwards does not contain the amount that constitutes establishing a meal, he may continue to eat on the basis of the Mezonot blessing he recited at the beginning. And at the end, since he ate the amount that constitutes establishing a meal, he should recite Birkat Ha-mazon (SA 168:6; MB 26).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Some explained these three interpretations as disagreeing, that is, according to each opinion only one of them is pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin (PHBK), and over the other two one recites Ha-motzi, since they are baked goods and their law is like bread. However, there is no decision as to whom the halakha follows, and because of the doubt SA 168:7 ruled that over each of them, if one did not establish a meal, one should recite Mezonot. So wrote the Baḥ, MA 18, Taz 6, Rabbi Akiva Eiger, and Dagul Me-revava; and see BHL 168:8, s.v. \"te'unim.\" In contrast, some explained that there is no disagreement here, but rather these are three examples of PHBK, the rule being that PHBK is a baked good that is generally eaten as dessert, and not like bread, which is the main part of the meal. This is implied by several Rishonim: Rashba, Meiri, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and Or Zaru'a. So too wrote Ma'amar Mordekhai 14, that it is possible to explain the view of SA this way. So too wrote Da'at Torah in the name of Beit Efrayim OḤ 12, and so wrote Arukh Ha-shulḥan 23. If we say that there is a disagreement among the three interpretations, seemingly, if one eats from two kinds, a kezayit of each, even if he does not establish a meal on them, since one of them is certainly bread, he would have to recite Birkat Ha-mazon at the end. However, in practice the ruling follows the opinion that there is no disagreement among the three interpretations, and all of them are considered Mezonot when one did not establish a meal on them. And even if we say that there is a disagreement among the three interpretations, as is implied by the Beit Yosef, one can say that after it was ruled, because of the doubt, that over all these kinds one recites Mezonot, in practice we regard all of them as PHBK and they have lost the status of bread. This is implied by MA 22 and the Levush 6."}]},{"slug":"10-06-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-03/","title_he":"ג – שיעור קביעות סעודה על מאפה מזונות","title_en":"The Amount That Constitutes Establishing a Meal on a Mezonot Baked Good","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, a person who wishes to eat bread, even a very small amount, must recite Ha-motzi, because bread is meant for establishing a meal, and even if one eats only a little of it, one recites an important blessing over it. But over other baked goods made from the five species of grain, such as cakes and biscuits, since people are not accustomed to establish a meal on them, one recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. Only when one establishes a meal on them are they elevated to the level of bread, and then, in order to eat them, one must wash one's hands with a blessing, and recite Ha-motzi at the beginning and Birkat Ha-mazon at the end.</p><p>The amount that constitutes establishing a meal is the amount that people customarily eat at a regular meal, such that the diner leaves it satiated and does not need to eat again until the next meal. It is impossible to set a volume measure for this, because there are airy baked goods that satiate only when one eats a large amount of them, and there are dense baked goods that satiate with a relatively small amount. Rather, everything depends on what is customary: if people are accustomed to be satiated by what he intends to eat, he should recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over it.</p><p>A person need not worry that perhaps he does not know what quantity people are accustomed to be satiated by, because whenever he eats an amount from which he is satiated in the way that he is satiated at a regular meal, he has established his meal on this eating, and he should recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. Only one who knows that he is noticeably different from other people, for example that he becomes satiated from much less or much more, must estimate according to what is customary for most people. And even then he should estimate according to what seems right to him, and need not conduct in-depth research for this purpose.</p><p>Elderly people or children who become satiated with a smaller amount need not estimate according to most people, but according to what is customary among the elderly or children.</p><p>Some say that the amount that constitutes establishing a meal is a volume of four eggs (kebeitzot) of the Mezonot baked good, and even though people are not usually satiated by such an amount, people are already accustomed to establish a meal on this amount. In practice, we do not rule according to this view, and one who eats a volume of four eggs of the Mezonot baked good, as long as he did not eat the amount from which people are accustomed to be satiated at a regular meal, should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. However, ideally (le-khatḥila), it is preferable not to eat an amount of four eggs, so as not to enter into a doubt. Rather, either he should eat less than a volume of four eggs, and then he recites Mezonot; or he should eat an amount from which people are accustomed to be satiated, and recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon according to all opinions.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In Berakhot 42a, regarding a Mezonot baked good, which is PHBK, they said: \"Whatever others establish a meal on, one must recite a blessing over it,\" meaning Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. There are two main approaches to defining \"establish a meal on it\": <b>The first approach</b>: establishing a meal is the amount from which people are satiated at a regular meal. So wrote the Tur and SA 168:6, and many others. The Rishonim disagreed as to the standard by which one estimates. According to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the Rosh, it is according to what is customary for most people. According to the Ra'avad and Rashba, if the one eating is accustomed to establish a meal on less than the amount on which others establish a meal, he recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over what he is accustomed to. SA 168:6 ruled like Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the Rosh. But regarding the elderly and children, BHL 168:6, s.v. \"af al pi,\" wrote that since it is their nature to be satiated by a smaller amount than is customary, we follow what is customary among them.<br><b>The second approach</b>: the amount of a pras. So wrote the Rema of Fano, Maharam ben Ḥaviv, and Beit David OḤ 82, according to what is explained in Eruvin 82b, that this is the amount of bread that people would eat at an average meal. (According to Rashi the amount of a pras is four eggs, and according to the Rambam three eggs.) And although most people are not satiated by this amount in the way they are satiated at a regular meal, in their opinion there is no need to be satiated from PHBK with the satiety of a regular meal; rather, it is sufficient that people eat of it the amount of bread eaten at a regular meal, which provides partial satiety, and if other foods are eaten with the baked goods as is customary, people are already fully satiated. And this is the meaning of the Gemara: \"Whatever others establish a meal on.\" Many of the later Sephardic poskim wrote in accordance with this opinion. Many of Sephardic descent in recent generations have been accustomed to calculate the measures of kezayit and kebeitza according to the weight of water (Ḥida in Maḥazik Berakha 168:6; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 45-46). According to their calculation, based on the measure of Rabbi Ḥayim Na'eh, the weight of four eggs is 224 grams, and by the precise calculation 200 grams. With baked goods, it turns out that a weight of 200 grams is generally about the volume of nine eggs (depending on the density of the baked good). And with such an amount people are already satiated without additional foods. Thus, in practice, by calculating by weight they equated the measure of four eggs with the measure of satiety. However, it is agreed that the measures are by volume (as explained below 10:6), and according to the proponents of the second approach, the amount that constitutes establishing a meal is eating the volume of a pras.<br>One should know that an error in this law is not so severe, since according to many poskim, if one recited Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya over bread, he has fulfilled his obligation (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 168:8; Ḥayei Adam 58:1; Responsa Yabia Omer vol. 2, OḤ 12; above 4:3). Likewise, if one recited Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over PHBK, he has fulfilled his obligation (Nishmat Adam 37:1; MB 208:75; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 168:43).<br>However, in a case of doubt, where according to the second approach one established a meal, having eaten a pras, and according to the first approach one did not establish one, since he was not satiated, one recites Al Ha-miḥya, since in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, and Al Ha-miḥya is one blessing as opposed to Birkat Ha-mazon, which includes four blessings (Derisha). Ideally, it is good not to eat an amount regarding which there is a doubt (and see MB 168:24, that there is no need to be concerned about the measure of three eggs).<br>One who eats PHBK in the volume of a pras which satiates him, but is in doubt whether others too are accustomed to be satiated by such an amount, should recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. First, there is room to say that it is a doubt regarding a Torah law and one should be stringent (MA and those who agree with him, as cited below in note 9). Second, one may add the opinion of the Ra'avad and Rashba (cited under the first approach), who hold that we follow the satiety of the one who is eating. Third, one may add the opinion that if one was satiated by the amount of bread that people are accustomed to eat at a satisfying meal together with other foods, he recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon (MB 168:24, based on MA and those who agree with him)."}]},{"slug":"10-06-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-04/","title_he":"ד – קביעות סעודה לאוכל מזונות עם עוד מאכלים","title_en":"Establishing a Meal When Eating Mezonot with Other Foods","body_en":"<p>One who intends to eat other foods together with the Mezonot baked good, such as salads and cheese, or meat and fish, should estimate everything together. If he eats from everything together an amount that satiates like a regular meal, the law of the Mezonot baked good is like that of bread. This is on condition that the Mezonot baked good is central to the meal, that is, that he eats of it at least a volume of four eggs. There is no difference whether the baked good is dense or airy – if it has a volume of four eggs, it is central to the meal. Only when the baked good has noticeable air pockets should one estimate the baked good without those pockets.</p><p>Thus there are <b>two conditions</b> here: <b>1)</b> That he eats of the Mezonot baked good at least a volume of four eggs. <b>2)</b> That he is satiated from everything he eats in the way people are accustomed to be satiated at a regular meal. If both conditions are fulfilled, he has established his meal on a Mezonot baked good, and he should wash his hands with a blessing and recite Ha-motzi over the baked good, and he should not recite blessings over the other foods, for they are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing as in every meal. At the end of the meal he should recite Birkat Ha-mazon.</p><p>Just as a person need not worry that perhaps he does not know how to estimate the amount of satiety, so too a person need not worry that perhaps he is mistaken in estimating a volume of four eggs. For from the fact that this measure was given over to people without measuring instruments, it is implied that they knew that sometimes people would err upward and sometimes downward. Rather, everything depends on the judgment of the person eating, and whenever he estimates that the Mezonot baked good he is eating contains a volume of four eggs, and that together with the other foods he will be satiated, he is establishing his meal on the Mezonot baked good, and he must recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon without concern. (The measure of one egg is about 50 ml, and a regular cup of leben (yogurt drink) contains a volume of four eggs.)<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"We learned in the previous note that there are two approaches to defining \"establish a meal on it.\" <b>According to the first approach</b>, it is the amount from which people are satiated at a regular meal. And MA 13 explained that this does not mean that the satiety must come from PHBK alone; rather, just as people are accustomed to eat bread and other foods at a meal, so too when one established his meal on PHBK, if he was satiated from PHBK and other foods, he recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over the PHBK, and exempts the other foods. Admittedly, according to Magen Giborim 7 and Arukh Ha-shulḥan 17, only if one ate a satiating amount of PHBK alone does he recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. However, most poskim wrote in accordance with the opinion of MA, among them Ginat Veradim OḤ 1:24, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 8, Ḥayei Adam 54:4, and MB 24. <b>According to the second approach</b>, the amount that constitutes establishing a meal is PHBK in the amount of a pras. And it makes no difference whether other foods are eaten with the PHBK or not. <b>One should know</b> that even according to the first approach, the PHBK must be central to the meal, and some learned from Eruvin 82b that the amount of bread at a meal is a pras. So wrote the Gra\"z (Rabbi Shneur Zalman of Liadi) in Seder Birkhot Ha-nehenin 2:3, that in order to establish a meal one must eat at least four eggs of PHBK (and in his Hilkhot 168:8 he wrote three eggs). However, according to Igrot Moshe OḤ vol. 3, 32, based on MA and MB 168:24, as long as one was satiated from the meal as a whole, and ate of the PHBK the amount of bread that people are accustomed to eat at a meal, even if it is less than the amount of a pras, this constitutes establishing a meal.<br>In practice, only if there is the amount that constitutes establishing a meal according to both approaches do we recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. Therefore, if one ate less than a volume of four eggs of PHBK with other foods, even though he was satiated, and according to most poskim (in explaining the first approach) this constitutes establishing a meal, since he did not eat PHBK in the amount of a pras according to Rashi, there is no establishing of a meal according to the second approach, and he should recite Mezonot. And likewise the reverse: one should not regard four eggs of PHBK as establishing a meal if one did not actually eat an amount that satiates like a regular meal.<br>But one need not be concerned about isolated opinions that increase the amount for establishing a meal further. Therefore, one need not be concerned about the opinion of Magen Giborim and Arukh Ha-shulḥan, who require that one be satiated from the PHBK alone. Nor need one be concerned about calculating the weight of four eggs of PHBK, because those who hold that the amount of four eggs is needed for establishing a meal agree that according to the basic law it refers to volume (see below 10:6, note 7). Therefore I wrote the volume calculation as primary, both for Sephardim and for Ashkenazim. Only one who knows that the custom of his forefathers was to calculate by weight in this law as well should continue his custom.<br>In addition to what I wrote above, that a person need not worry that perhaps he does not know how to estimate a volume of four eggs, one should add that we have already learned that according to the Rambam and those who agree with him, the amount for eating a pras is a volume of three eggs. And according to Igrot Moshe and those who agree with him, even if one eats less than a volume of three eggs of the baked good, since he was satiated he should recite Ha-motzi. Therefore, even if he errs in estimating four eggs, it turns out that he ate the amount of three eggs, which is a sufficient amount according to the great majority of poskim.<br>Regarding the elderly and children, who are accustomed to eat less to be satiated, there is room to say that eating PHBK should be the amount of three eggs, and that they be satiated together with the other foods."}]},{"slug":"10-06-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-05/","title_he":"ה – כיצד לצאת ממצבי ספק","title_en":"How to Get Out of Situations of Doubt","body_en":"<p>Ideally, in order to get out of the doubt, it is proper to plan one's eating from the outset taking the two conditions into account: either one eats four eggs' worth of the Mezonot food and, together with the other foods, a satiating amount, and then according to all opinions he recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. Or one eats less than a volume of four eggs of Mezonot and, together with the other foods, less than a satiating amount, and then according to all opinions he recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, and over the other foods he recites their own blessings.</p><p>If nevertheless he finds himself in doubt, in that he cannot decide whether what he intends to eat contains a satiating amount; or he knows that together with the other foods he is eating a satiating amount, but the amount of Mezonot is certainly less than a volume of four eggs – since he is in doubt, he should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, and over the other foods he should likewise recite their own blessings. And it is better that he finish eating the Mezonot food before he eats a satiating amount, and recite Al Ha-miḥya over it, and thereby all the other foods he eats afterwards do not combine with the Mezonot baked good, and according to all opinions he recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya over it, and over the other foods he recites their own blessings.</p><p>Here is the place to note a common mistake: sometimes people invite their relatives and friends to a party and wish to honor them with a satiating meal, but since they do not want to trouble them to wash their hands and recite Birkat Ha-mazon, instead of bread they bring crackers and cakes, and also serve them meat, fish, cold cuts, salads, pashtidot and compote. But according to halakha, since at such a meal people are accustomed to eat a volume of four eggs of the Mezonot baked good and also to be satiated, one must wash one's hands and recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over the Mezonot baked good. And even when people do not eat a volume of four eggs, as we have learned, since they are satiated, they enter into a doubt. And sometimes sweet rolls are served there, and people think their blessing is Mezonot. They make two mistakes: 1) Generally these rolls are considered bread, and even one who eats a little of them must recite Ha-motzi (as explained in the next section). 2) Generally people eat more than a volume of four eggs of them. Therefore, one who wishes to give his guests a meal that will satiate them should properly prepare bread there, and thus everyone will know that they must wash their hands and recite Ha-motzi, and thereby they will all merit to recite at the end the long Birkat Ha-mazon, which is a Torah commandment and contains four important blessings.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Many err in estimating a volume of four eggs, but in truth it is not as large as people think. For example, a large croissant contains more than a volume of four eggs. Likewise, four slices of cake contain a volume of four eggs. If so, generally at these meals people eat a volume of four eggs of PHBK. And even one who eats a volume of three eggs of PHBK enters a significant doubt, since according to the Rambam and many poskim the amount of a pras is three eggs, and he must recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over the Mezonot food. And even if he eats two eggs' worth of PHBK, according to Igrot Moshe, cited in the previous note, under the first approach, since he was satiated he must recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. Therefore, in order to get out of these doubts, it is proper to serve actual bread there. And even if bread was not served, it is proper to recite Ha-motzi over the Mezonot baked good and eat a volume of four eggs of it."}]},{"slug":"10-06-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-06/","title_he":"ו – חלה מתוקה","title_en":"Sweet Ḥalla","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, a Mezonot baked good (pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin) has an intermediate status: if one eats it between meals, one recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, and if one establishes a meal on it, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. But over bread, even when one eats a little, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. The question is where the boundary between bread and a Mezonot baked good lies.</p><p>The rule is that whatever people are accustomed to establish a meal on has the law of bread, whereas whatever people are accustomed to eat between meals, as a sweet or to somewhat calm one's hunger, has the law of a Mezonot baked good. Accordingly, the law of sweet ḥalla and sweet rolls is like bread, since people are accustomed to eat them at meals, and are not accustomed to serve them as a snack between meals. People are also accustomed to eat them with various salads, or to make a sandwich of them with cheese or tuna; thus they treat them as bread. Therefore, even when one eats a little of them, one must recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over them. In our language, whatever is called bread or ḥalla or a roll – people are accustomed to establish a meal on it. In contrast, if a baked good is called \"cake\" or \"cracker\" and the like – people are not accustomed to establish a meal on it. Over a baked good about which there is a doubt whether it is bread or cake, one recites Mezonot. One who wishes to get out of the doubt should establish a meal on it and recite Ha-motzi (as explained above, sections 3-4), or eat it within a bread meal for the purpose of satiety, and then the Ha-motzi blessing he recites over the bread will exempt it according to all opinions.</p><p>Admittedly, many of Sephardic descent were accustomed to recite Mezonot over sweet ḥalla, because in the past people were not accustomed to serve it at a meal, but rather to serve slices of sweet ḥalla as refreshments between meals. However, in practice, since today ḥalla or a sweet roll is intended for meals and not as dessert between meals, one should always recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over them.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"Seemingly this is a disagreement between SA and Rema (168:7): according to SA, whenever there is a sweet taste in the dough one recites Mezonot, and according to Rema, only when the sweet taste is dominant does one recite Mezonot. So it is written in several books of our time (see Piskei Teshuvot 168:10). However, in the Beit Yosef he wrote his reason explicitly: \"Since it is not the way of people to establish their meal on it.\" It turns out that they did not disagree at all; rather, in the past, in the places of those of Sephardic descent, people were not accustomed to serve a baked good with sweetness as bread, whereas in Ashkenaz they were. And this is the basic rule: whatever people are accustomed to establish a meal on – its blessings are like bread. So wrote Rishonim and Aḥaronim, among them: Rav Hai Gaon, Rashi, Rashba, Ritva, Beit David, MA 19, Pri Megadim, MB 35-36, and BHL on paragraph 17, Sdei Ḥemed, Asefat Dinim, Ma'arekhet Berakhot 1:10, and Yaskil Avdi OḤ vol. 1, 9:6. Since this is the basic rule, consequently everything depends on how it is perceived in people's eyes, and therefore the shape of the baked good also affects its blessing. Thus it is possible that dough with a certain amount of sugar, if baked in the shape of a roll, will be considered bread, and if made in the shape of a cake, will be considered cake.<br>As for the custom of those of Sephardic descent: after SA recorded the custom that was accepted in the past, to recite Mezonot over a baked good with sweetness, like cake, many continued to follow it even when it was clear that the custom had changed, as in the case of sweet ḥalla, on which people customarily establish a meal (Ginat Veradim). So too wrote Or Le-Tzion vol. 2, 12:4, but he added that a God-fearing person should eat it only within a meal, because according to the basic law one ought to recite Ha-motzi over it. Nevertheless, there are those who still customarily recite Mezonot over sweet ḥalla outside the framework of a meal, and one should not protest against them if this is the definite custom of their family. Ideally, it is preferable that they not eat sweet ḥalla outside a meal, so as not to enter a doubt regarding blessings.<br>The Aḥaronim disagreed about the law of dough in which most of the liquid is milk or fruit juice but there is almost no difference between its taste and the taste of regular dough. According to Taz 7 and MB 33, since the sweet taste is not noticeable more than the taste of the dough, and people are accustomed to establish a meal on it, its blessing is Ha-motzi. Some say that if most of the liquids are fruit juice or milk and the taste is also somewhat noticeable, its blessing is Mezonot (so wrote Kaf Ha-ḥayim 58, in explaining the view of Rema, and so is implied by the Gra\"z in Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 11 and Seder Birkhot Ha-nehenin 2:7). According to the Maharsham in Da'at Torah 168:7, if most of the liquids are fruit juice or milk, even though their taste is not noticeable in the baked good, its blessing is Mezonot. In practice, it seems that the essence of the law depends on the purpose of the baked good, as explained above according to the overwhelming majority of poskim. Therefore, when it comes to ḥalla or a roll, even if most of the liquids are not water, since they are baked for the purpose of establishing a meal, their blessing is Ha-motzi. And it is possible that even according to the Maharsham, if dough kneaded with fruit juice was baked in order to establish a meal on it, its blessing is Ha-motzi. And so it is explicit in the words of the Gra\"z cited above."}]},{"slug":"10-06-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-07/","title_he":"ז – מצה","title_en":"Matza","body_en":"<p>When one establishes a meal on matza, it is clear that one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over it. The question is what the law is when one eats matza not within the framework of establishing a meal.</p><p>Some say that its blessing is Mezonot, because one of the definitions of pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin is a hard baked good that a person crunches with his teeth, such as crackers and matza crackers (matziyot). And since our matzot too are hard – their blessing is Mezonot. Admittedly, on Pesaḥ, when matza serves as bread, even one who eats a little of it recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon, but during the rest of the year one recites Mezonot over it (Ginat Veradim).</p><p>In contrast, many poskim wrote that since matza serves as bread on Pesaḥ, throughout the year its law is like bread. In addition, there is a difference between the purpose of matza and the purpose of crackers and matza crackers, for people are accustomed to establish a meal on matza, whereas matza crackers and crackers are eaten as dessert and as a snack. Therefore, even when one eats matza casually, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over it. And this is the custom of those of Ashkenazic descent.</p><p>The custom of those of Sephardic descent is to take into account the opinion that throughout the year the law of matza is like that of crackers and matza crackers, whose blessing is Mezonot. Admittedly, ideally it is desirable to get out of the doubt and eat matza within a bread meal, exempting it with the Ha-motzi blessing. But one who wishes to eat matza not within the framework of a meal, according to the custom of those of Sephardic descent, should recite Mezonot (Yeḥaveh Da'at 3:12). And on the night after Pesaḥ and on Isru Ḥag of Pesaḥ, as long as people have not been able to buy bread, even according to the custom of those of Sephardic descent one should recite Ha-motzi over matza, because at that time matza is considered bread (Or Le-Tzion 12:3).<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The Ashkenazic custom is clear, and so it is cited in Tzitz Eliezer 11:19. The Sephardic custom is explained in Yeḥaveh Da'at 3:12. Admittedly, there were Sephardic poskim who ruled always to recite Ha-motzi over matza, and so wrote Knesset Ha-gedola and Beit David. But the custom is to recite Mezonot over thin matzot, as cited in Maḥazik Berakha 158:5. And Or Le-Tzion vol. 2, 12:3 wrote that although according to the basic law it would be proper to recite Ha-motzi, since people are accustomed to eat it as bread, the custom is to recite Mezonot, and ideally it is preferable not to eat it outside the framework of a meal. According to Rav Eliyahu, even according to the Sephardic custom, until the 14th of Iyar, the time of Pesaḥ Sheni, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over matza. As for the amount that constitutes establishing a meal, if we estimate according to the weight of four eggs – 200 grams, it would turn out that one would need to eat about seven matzot, which people are not accustomed to eat even at large meals. However, we have already learned (note 3) that the main approach is to calculate by volume, and slightly less than three regular matzot contain a volume of four eggs. Therefore, whoever ate about three matzot and was satiated must, according to all opinions, recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon."}]},{"slug":"10-06-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-08/","title_he":"ח – מאפה ממולא בגבינה או בשר, בורקס","title_en":"A Baked Good Filled with Cheese or Meat; Bourekas","body_en":"<p>A question has arisen regarding the law of baked goods whose dough is like bread dough, and which contain a filling of cheese or meat.</p><p>Some say that their blessing is Mezonot, for one of the definitions of the baked goods called pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin is that they are filled, and since a filling of cheese or meat was put inside the bread, people are not accustomed to establish a meal on it, and its blessing is Mezonot (Tanya; Taz 168:20).</p><p>And some say that only kinds of baked goods filled with sweet things, such as sugar and poppy seeds, are considered pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin, whose blessing is Mezonot. But if the filling is of the kinds of foods that people customarily eat together with bread, such as meat or cheese, people are accustomed to establish a meal on it, and the law of the baked good is like that of bread, and its blessing is Ha-motzi (Riaz; Shela; and so is implied by SA 168:17).</p><p>In practice, the law depends on the purpose of the baked good. If it is a baked good on which people are accustomed to establish a meal, its law is like bread, and even when one eats a little of it, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. And if it is a baked good that people are accustomed to eat as a snack between meals, its blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, and only when one establishes a meal on it does one recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. Generally, a large baked good is intended for establishing a meal, and a small one is intended as a snack. But over bourekas, even when it is large, as long as one did not establish a meal, one recites Mezonot, since its dough too is very different from bread, as oil is mixed into it.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"According to the Tanya and Taz 168:20, the blessing over anything filled is Mezonot, including if it is filled with meat or cheese, and so wrote Eliya Rabba and Magen Giborim. In contrast, the opinion of the Rid and his grandson the Riaz is that since people are accustomed to eat bread together with meat and cheese, even if they were baked with a filling of meat or cheese, their blessing is Ha-motzi. And this is the plain meaning of SA 168:17, and in accordance with the main explanation of MA 44. So too wrote the Shela, Ma'amar Mordekhai, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 168:10, and Halikhot Olam (Pinḥas 12). According to Ben Ish Ḥai (Pinḥas 20) and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 137, because of the doubt one should rule like the Taz, since in a doubt between Mezonot and Ha-motzi, one recites Mezonot (see note 2). And BHL explained that they did not disagree, for in truth, if it is a baked good on which people are accustomed to establish a meal, even over a little of it one recites Ha-motzi, and if people are not accustomed to establish a meal on it, over a little of it one recites Mezonot. If so, in practice, when people are accustomed to establish a meal on this baked good – its blessing is Ha-motzi, for that is the opinion of most poskim, and perhaps even all of them, according to BHL. And if people are not accustomed to establish a meal on it, then according to BHL, in the opinion of most poskim its blessing is Mezonot, and even if according to the Shela and those who agree with him its blessing is Ha-motzi, because of the doubt one should recite Mezonot."}]},{"slug":"10-06-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-09/","title_he":"ט – פיצה","title_en":"Pizza","body_en":"<p>In the vast majority of cases the law of pizza is like that of bread, because pizza dough is like bread dough. And although yellow cheese, tomato paste and various spices are placed on the dough, they do not change the law of the base, which is bread. Thus the law of pizza is like that of a slice of bread on which various spreads have been spread, that even if one ate a little of it, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over it.</p><p>But if it is a special pizza whose flour was kneaded with egg or milk, and as a result the taste of its baked dough is different, its law is like that of pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin, and when one does not establish a meal on it, its blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. And when one establishes a meal on it, its blessing is Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. As we have learned (in sections 3-4), there are two conditions for establishing a meal: first, that one eats of the Mezonot baked good a volume of at least four eggs; and second, that one is satiated as is usual at a regular meal.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"When the dough is kneaded with water its blessing is Ha-motzi, as the Shela and most poskim wrote (and it is possible that even the Taz would agree to this, since here we are not dealing with a pocket but with something like a spread slice; Or Le-Tzion vol. 2, 14:5). Admittedly, some say that a single portion of pizza eaten in the street is not intended for establishing a meal, and although its dough is like bread dough, as long as it is eaten as a snack between meals its blessing is Mezonot (see Ve-zot Ha-berakha p. 230, in the name of Rav Feinstein). However, in practice one should recite Ha-motzi over it, and all the more so today, when the custom of eating pizza has changed, for in the past it was more common to eat it casually, whereas today many are accustomed to establish a meal on it, and thereby the ruling that its blessing is Ha-motzi has been strengthened. Only if the dough was kneaded with fruit juice and its taste is different is its law like PHBK, and as long as one did not establish a meal on the pizza, one should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya."}]},{"slug":"10-06-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-10/","title_he":"י – על תבשיל לעולם מברכים מזונות","title_en":"Over a Cooked Dish One Always Recites Mezonot","body_en":"<p>Everything we have learned, that sometimes one recites Ha-motzi over a Mezonot baked good, is because we are dealing with a baked good. But over cooked dishes of grain, such as noodles and ptitim, and likewise porridge made of grain, even if one eats a large quantity of them and establishes a meal on them – one always recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. The reason the Sages distinguished between baked goods and cooked dishes is that all kinds of baked goods resemble bread to a certain extent, both in their form and in their preparation by baking. Therefore, when one eats a Mezonot baked good between meals – one recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, and when one establishes a meal on it – one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. But cooked dishes are entirely different from bread, and therefore even when one establishes a meal on them, their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.</p><p>The same law applies to Bissli and soup croutons (shkedei marak), for since they are made by deep-frying in oil, they are considered cooked, and even if one establishes a meal on them – their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. The same law applies to sufganiyot (doughnuts): since they are cooked in deep oil – their law is like a cooked dish, and even if one establishes a meal on them their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya (and if they are baked sufganiyot, their law is like pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin).<sup>9</sup></p><p>The same law applies to kugel: since the noodles are cooked first, even though the kugel is baked afterwards, since it did not take on the form of bread through the baking, and also beforehand the noodles were fit to eat, the law of the kugel remains that of a cooked dish, so that even if one establishes a meal on it – its blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"See Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 11, note 12, that according to Rabbeinu Tam, one who rolls out a thick dough, even if afterwards he cooked it, since the obligation of ḥalla (separating a portion of dough) takes effect at the time of kneading, it became obligated in ḥalla. And according to most poskim, since he intended to cook it, it is exempt from ḥalla. As for blessings, according to Rabbeinu Tam this cooked food is considered a baked good, and if it was kneaded with water its blessing is Ha-motzi, and if it has a taste of oil or sweetness to the extent that it is considered pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin, its blessing is Ha-motzi only if one establishes a meal on it. And according to most poskim, since it is cooked, its blessing is always Mezonot. Accordingly, even for one who establishes a meal on sufganiyot, since they are fried in deep oil, their blessing is Mezonot. SA 168:13 wrote the opinion of most poskim as the unattributed ruling and mentioned Rabbeinu Tam as the opinion of \"some who disagree,\" and wrote that in order to get out of the disagreement, one who wishes to establish a meal on sufganiyot should first recite Ha-motzi over bread and at the end Birkat Ha-mazon. Thus far his words. But if one did not eat bread, he should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. Admittedly, according to MA 168:34 and Eliya Rabba, since one was satiated from the sufganiyot, it is a doubt regarding a Torah law, and one should be stringent like Rabbeinu Tam and recite Birkat Ha-mazon. However, Dagul Me-revava and Magen Giborim wrote that even in a doubt regarding a Torah law one should recite Me'ein Shalosh, because according to Torah law one fulfills the obligation with Me'ein Shalosh. Beit Meir and Pri Megadim further explained that the Beit Yosef was not concerned here about a doubt regarding a Torah law, because most poskim disagreed with Rabbeinu Tam, and therefore he wrote that it is good to be stringent only for a God-fearing person. Therefore, even if one established a meal on sufganiyot, he should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.<br>Above, 3:8, note 9, it was explained that ideally it is preferable not to eat sufganiyot within a meal, because perhaps they are intended as dessert and are not exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. Admittedly, in practice one does not recite a blessing on eating them within a meal, since they are included in the meal. In that case it is proper to have in mind explicitly, in the Ha-motzi blessing, to exempt the sufganiyot that one will eat during the meal."}]},{"slug":"10-06-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-11/","title_he":"יא – מאפים שונים (מלאווח, כובאנה, בלינצ'ס, פנקייק וופל)","title_en":"Various Baked Goods (Malawaḥ, Kubaneh, Blintzes, Pancakes, Waffles)","body_en":"<p>Malawaḥ is a kind of puff pastry, which is made in many layers, with margarine spread between one layer and the next. Afterwards the malawaḥ is fried in a pan with a little oil or baked in the oven. Since it contains a lot of margarine, even though it somewhat resembles a pita, its law is like pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin: if one establishes a meal on it, one recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon, and if one ate it casually between meals, one recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.</p><p>As we have learned, there are two rules in defining the establishing of a meal: first, that it contains a volume of four eggs, and second, that the diner is satiated, and it makes no difference whether the satiety comes from the Mezonot baked good alone or from the baked good together with other foods. One portion of malawaḥ contains close to a volume of four eggs, but people are not satiated by it as they are satiated at a regular meal. Therefore, one who intends to eat one portion should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, and one who intends to eat two portions, or one portion with other foods, in the amount from which people are accustomed to be satiated at a regular meal, should wash his hands and recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon.</p><p>One who intends to eat slightly more than one portion – it is doubtful whether he will eat the amount that constitutes establishing a meal; therefore, one who knows that he will be satiated by it should recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon, and one who is in doubt should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.</p><p>The same law applies to kubaneh and jaḥnun, which are dough-based baked goods baked inside a pot: since they contain a lot of oil, they are not considered bread, and therefore one who eats them between meals recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. And if he established a meal on them, he recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. The same is the law of a tortilla: its blessing is Mezonot, and if one established a meal on it, its law is like bread.</p><p>There are foods made from a soft batter on a pan, such as pancakes, laḥoḥ, blintzes, Belgian waffles and French crêpes, and since their final form is like the form of a baked good, their law is like pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin: one who establishes a meal on them washes his hands with a blessing, and recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon.</p><p>One who is in doubt whether he will eat of one of these foods an amount from which people are satiated should recite Mezonot; and one who estimates that he will be satiated but is in doubt whether it is an amount from which most people are satiated should recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon (as explained at the end of note 2).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Even though malawaḥ is fried in a pan, since the frying is with a little oil to prevent sticking to the pan, it should not be regarded as a cooked dish whose blessing is always Mezonot; rather, its law is like a baked good, so that if one established a meal on it, its blessing is Ha-motzi. Even kubaneh and jaḥnun, which contain a lot of oil, are not cooked in the pot but baked in it, and their law is like a baked good. (This is not as written in Shulḥan Arukh Ha-mekutzar vol. 1, 34:9, that their law is like a cooked dish, but rather in accordance with the words of Rav Kafiḥ in his siddur, who regarded them as baked goods.) Only if much liquid remains in the jaḥnun and it is soft like a cooked dish is its law like a cooked dish.<br>A baked good made from a soft, very thin and flimsy batter (tri'ta in SA 168:15), since it does not have the form of bread, even one who establishes a meal on it recites Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya (see Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 11:12, note 14). As an example of this, a single sheet of wafer was cited (see MB 168:38). Therefore some ruled that a regular wafer does not have the law of PHBK, but rather its blessing is always Mezonot. However, since a wafer is composed of many layers, in practice it has the form of a baked good and its law is like PHBK.<br><b>Laḥoḥ and blintzes</b> have the law of PHBK: if one established a meal on them – their law is like bread, and if one did not – their blessing is Mezonot. Admittedly, some are accustomed to regard laḥoḥ as a cooked dish whose blessing is always Mezonot; however, it seems that it should not be regarded as a cooked dish, for in a cooked dish the food is cooked within liquid, whereas with laḥoḥ the entire soft batter turns into a baked good and its appearance is like a baked good, and therefore its law is not like tri'ta. The same is the law of blintzes. The law of a <b>tortilla</b> too is like that of PHBK; and although its dough is more similar to bread, being a thick dough without much oil or sugar, since it is thin and intended for casual eating and for holding a filling, its law is like PHBK."}]},{"slug":"10-06-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-12/","title_he":"יב – לחם מטוגן, קרוטונים, מצות מטוגנות","title_en":"Fried Bread, Croutons, Fried Matzot","body_en":"<p>As is known, because of the importance of bread, the Sages established special blessings for it, Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon. The question is what one recites over kinds of bread that were cooked or fried: does one still recite Ha-motzi over them, or have they descended from their stature so that one recites Mezonot over them?</p><p>The rule is that if the pieces of bread that were cooked are larger than a kezayit (the volume of half an egg), even if their form changed and they do not look like bread, since they are larger than a kezayit, the law of bread remains upon them and their blessing is Ha-motzi. And if the pieces of bread that were cooked are smaller than a kezayit, even if the form of bread remained upon them, they have descended from their stature, and even if one establishes a meal on them, their blessing is Mezonot. Therefore, over croutons manufactured in factories one recites Mezonot, because they are pieces of bread smaller than a kezayit that were cooked in deep oil.</p><p>But one who prepares croutons from pieces of bread by baking them in the oven – even if they are smaller than a kezayit, since they did not undergo cooking and their form remained the form of bread – even over a single piece one recites Ha-motzi, and if one ate a kezayit of them one recites Birkat Ha-mazon at the end.</p><p>Breadcrumbs smaller than a kezayit that were consolidated and compressed or baked with other ingredients to create a cake or a patty – since they are smaller than a kezayit and do not have the form of bread – their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.</p><p>One who fries slices of bread with egg – since the pieces are larger than a kezayit, according to all opinions he recites Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon over them. And if one fried pieces smaller than a kezayit and the form of bread remained upon them, there is a doubt about their blessing. For there is a doubt whether frying in a pan is considered cooking, in which case their blessing is Mezonot, or baking, in which case their blessing is Ha-motzi. In order to get out of the doubt, it is proper to eat them within a bread meal. And if one eats them by themselves, because of the doubt he should recite Mezonot.</p><p>Regarding fried pieces of matza (matza brei) too there is a doubt, since the form of matza remained upon them. Then, if frying is considered baking, their blessing is Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon; and if it is considered cooking, even if one establishes a meal on them, their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya. In order to get out of the doubt, it is proper first to eat a kezayit of bread or matza, recite Ha-motzi, and thereby also exempt the fried matza, and at the end to recite Birkat Ha-mazon. And one who does not wish to establish a meal on the fried matza, but only to taste some of it between meals, should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya over it.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The law of fried pieces of bread smaller than a kezayit depends on the status of frying in a pan (MB 168:56; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 52). Some say that its law is like cooking (Gra\"z, Seder Birkhot Ha-nehenin 2:12; Ḥayei Adam 54:6), in which case their blessing is Mezonot, and some say that its law is like baking, in which case their blessing is Ha-motzi (MA 168:36; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 44). The law is explained above.<br>The law of <b>matza brei (fried pieces of matza)</b> depends on this disagreement. <b>If one did not eat a satiating amount</b>, he should recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, as in every doubt. And although from the standpoint of enhancing the mitzva (hiddur) it is preferable to eat it within a meal, since the doubt leans more toward the Mezonot blessing, one who wishes may ideally recite Mezonot over it. The reason is that we have already learned (section 7) that according to the custom of those of Sephardic descent the blessing over matza is Mezonot all year round, and all the more so over fried matza (which is possibly cooked) one should recite Mezonot. And even those of Ashkenazic descent may add this reasoning. And even on Pesaḥ, when even according to the Sephardic custom one recites Ha-motzi over matza, it seems that with fried matza one should lean toward the Mezonot blessing, for since it was fried, it is reasonable that it has returned to its year-round status, when some say its blessing is Mezonot. <b>And if one ate a satiating amount of it</b>, the doubt is greater; see note 9, that the Aḥaronim disagreed whether we say here that a doubt regarding a Torah law is treated stringently, so that one recites Birkat Ha-mazon (MA and most poskim), or since all the elements of Birkat Ha-mazon are mentioned in Me'ein Shalosh, one recites Me'ein Shalosh (Dagul Me-revava). In practice, it is preferable to get out of the doubt and recite the blessing over bread or matza. And when there is no such possibility, and one intends to establish a meal on it, since he regarded it as bread – he should wash his hands with a blessing and recite Ha-motzi and Birkat Ha-mazon."}]},{"slug":"10-06-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-06-13/","title_he":"יג – גרגירי דגן חיים, שלווה, גרנולה ופריכיות חיטה","title_en":"Raw Grain Kernels, Shalva, Granola and Wheat Crackers","body_en":"<p>The usual way of eating the five species of grain is as a baked good or a cooked dish, and then their blessing is Ha-motzi or Mezonot. But one who eats kernels raw, or roasted, or slightly cooked, recites Ha-adama, for since he did not eat them in accordance with their stature, they are like other kinds of legumes, over which one recites Ha-adama.<sup>12</sup></p><p>However, if an important food was prepared from them, such as wheat crackers (crispbreads), shalva (puffed wheat) and granola, since they are fully prepared, and people are accustomed to eat them as an important food that is not inferior to cooked grain dishes, their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Regarding the after-blessing over grain kernels, a doubt arose. According to most poskim one should recite Borei Nefashot, in accordance with the opinion of the Ritva, Ra'ah, Meiri, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and Ra'avad, and so is implied by the Rambam and SA 208:4. So too wrote MA, MB 18, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 9 and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 29. However, Tosafot were in doubt whether perhaps one must recite Me'ein Shalosh over them, and therefore wrote that it is proper not to eat them by themselves. So too wrote the Rosh and Rashba. Therefore, because of the doubt, it is preferable not to eat them by themselves, and if one ate them by themselves, he should recite Borei Nefashot (and see Birkat Hashem vol. 2, 2:6-8 , that some are concerned about the doubt regarding blessings)."},{"n":13,"en":"Admittedly, SA 208:4 ruled that over grain that is raw, or made into roasted kernels, or slightly cooked (shaluk), if the kernels are whole, one recites Ha-adama. And if one removed their husks and they softened somewhat in cooking, from the Rambam it appears that their blessing is Mezonot, and according to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona their blessing is Ha-adama (Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 19-20). However, all this applies when they have not reached the state in which people are accustomed to eat them. But when the grain was prepared until it reached the state in which people are accustomed to eat it as an important and accepted food, such as shalva, granola and crackers, their law is like grain eaten in its important manner, whose blessing is Mezonot. For it appears from the Rishonim and poskim that all kinds of grain eaten in an important and accepted manner of eating have the blessing Mezonot (Tosafot Berakhot 37a, s.v. \"ha-koses\"; Rashba ibid. 36a; Tur 208:4; Beit Yosef 208:7; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 9). So too wrote Responsa Sho'el Ve-nish'al 3:103-104. Admittedly, there were poskim of our time who were uncertain about this, because they resemble grain that is roasted or slightly cooked. However, in practice there is no room for concern, and their blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, for it is clear that they are considered an important food that has been fully prepared, and they are in no respect inferior to other Mezonot dishes, and some even establish their breakfast on them. So ruled Or Le-Tzion vol. 2, 46:43 regarding granola, and Rav Mordekhai Eliyahu regarding wheat crackers. In any case, there is no concern of a blessing in vain here, since the Mezonot blessing exempts anything nourishing, all the more so when it is of a grain species. And the Al Ha-miḥya blessing too exempts, at least after the fact (be-di'avad), foods made from grain species, just as it exempts rice, which resembles grain, as cited below 10, note 4.<br>When one eats shalva and granola with a little milk, the Mezonot blessing exempts the milk; and if one adds milk beyond what is needed to flavor the shalva and granola, one recites Mezonot over the shalva and granola, and Shehakol separately over the milk. Over the almonds in the granola one need not recite a blessing, since they are a minority; and if one wishes to be meticulous and recite a blessing over them, he should separate one of them, recite the blessing over it and eat it by itself."}]}]});
